Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS — Enable real-time content preview so that when an editor modifies content in Contentstack CMS, the preview site reflects those changes without a full page reload. This integrates with the existing useContent hook and Contentstack Live Preview SDK.

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single point where ALL content flows into the React component tree. It currently only serves static defaultContent baked at build time via getStaticProps/readFromFs, with no mechanism to receive or merge real-time content updates from Contentstack's Live Preview SDK. Every component using useContent → useContentstackContext reads from here, so without live-preview integration at this provider, no downstream component can ever reflect CMS edits in real time.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the useMemo with useState initialized from defaultContent, and add a useEffect that subscribes to Contentstack's onEntryChange callback (from @contentstack/live-preview-utils) to merge incoming live-preview entry data into the content state. The provider must also accept a livePreviewConfig prop (containing stack details, environment, and preview token) so the SDK can be initialized. When onEntryChange fires with an updated entry, deep-merge it into the matching key within the existing content state (page, header, footer, etc.) so all consumers of useContent immediately re-render with the previewed value. This is the minimal change: one useState replacing useMemo, one useEffect for the subscription, and one new optional prop — no re-architecture needed.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- With the page open in Contentstack preview mode, when a tester edits a content field in the CMS, the updated text or value appears on the rendered page without the tester manually reloading the browser.
- When the page is opened in Contentstack preview mode, the tester sees the page display the draft or edited CMS content rather than the published version.
- When the page is opened outside preview mode in a normal browser session, the tester sees the published content and no edits made in the CMS appear on the page until they are published.
- After a tester edits a piece of content in the CMS while in preview mode, every location on the rendered page where that content is displayed shows the updated value.
- If the live preview connection fails or is interrupted while in preview mode, the tester sees the page continue to display the last available content, with no error screen, blank page, or broken layout.
- When the page is rendered, the tester sees the correct published content values displayed in each content area of the page.

